==Nirvana==
[[File:Kailash north.JPG|thumb|left|Mount Kailash or Ashtapad, the [[Nirvana]] place of Rishabhdeva.]]

Rishabhanatha is said to have preached the principles of Jainism far and wide.{{sfn|Natubhai Shah|2004|p=19}}{{sfn|Cort|2010|p=115}} He is suggested to have attained ''Nirvana'' or ''moksha'', destroying all four of his ''aghati-[[Karma in Jainism|karma]]''.{{sfn|Dalal|2010b|pp=183, 368}} This is marked as liberation of his soul from the endless cycle of rebirths to stay eternally at ''siddhaloka''. His death is believed in Jainism to have occurred on ''Ashtapada'' (also known as [[Mount Kailash]]) on the fourteenth day of [[Magha (month)|Magha]] Krishna (Hindu Calendar).{{sfn|Cort|2010|p=115}}{{sfn|Natubhai Shah|2004|pp=20-21}}{{sfn|Cort|2010|p=135}} His total age at that time is suggested to be 84 ''lakh'' ''purva'' years, with three years and eight and a half months remaining of the third e''ra''.{{sfn|Vijay K. Jain|2015|p=182}}{{sfn|Finegan|1952|p=190}} According to medieval era Jain texts, Rishabhanatha performed asceticism for millions of years, then returned to Ashtapada where he fasted and performing inner meditation to his moksha. They further state that Indra came with his fellow gods from the heavens after that, to perform rituals of the place from where Rishabhanatha attained ''[[moksha]]''.{{sfn|Cort|2010|pp=121–122}}

Rishabhanatha's successor, [[Ajitanatha]], is said to have been born 50 ''lakh crore sagara'' after him.{{sfn|Finegan|1952|p=190}}

